; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Adding Waves Together
; CATEGORY: Science & Simulation
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     waves, interference, beats, superposition, animation
; SUMMARY:  Two traveling waves with slightly different wavelengths are added point by point, and their sum shows the pattern called beats.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   The blue wave (top) and the red wave (middle) have almost the same
;   wavelength, and both slide to the right. The black wave (bottom)
;   is their sum: at every x it is the blue height plus the red height.
;   Where the two waves are in step they add up to a big wave; where
;   one is up while the other is down they cancel out. The sum grows
;   and shrinks in groups called beats. The thin grey curves show the
;   outline, or "envelope", of the beats.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run and watch. Press Stop to end. Change WAVES.A and WAVES.B
;   (how many wiggles fit across the screen) and run again.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * superposition: waves that overlap simply add their heights
;   * constructive and destructive interference
;   * beats: two nearly equal notes make a slow wobble in loudness,
;     which musicians use to tune their instruments
;   * computing a whole curve from a formula with a FOR loop
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Make WAVES.B equal WAVES.A. When are there no beats at all?
;   * Make the two waves move in opposite directions (standing wave).
;   * Give the red wave half the height of the blue one.
; ============================================================

make "waves.a 20       ; wiggles across the screen for the blue wave
make "waves.b 23       ; and for the red wave
make "height 40
make "width 720

; Height of a wave at screen position x, at time t. Each wave slides
; right as t grows because we subtract the time from the angle.
to wave.a :x :t
  output :height * sin (360 * :waves.a * :x / :width - :t)
end

to wave.b :x :t
  output :height * sin (360 * :waves.b * :x / :width - :t * 1.15)
end

; Draw a curve at height :middle. :which is the name of a procedure
; that gives the height at each x (wave.a, wave.b or sum.ab).
to draw.curve :which :middle :t :color
  setpc :color setpensize 2
  penup
  for [x 0 :width 4] [
    ; INVOKE runs the procedure named in :which; the parentheses are
    ; needed because we give it two inputs instead of one
    setxy :x - 360 :middle + (invoke :which :x :t)
    pendown
  ]
  penup
end

to sum.ab :x :t
  output (wave.a :x :t) + (wave.b :x :t)
end

; The envelope: the biggest the sum can be at each x. It comes from
; the difference between the two waves, which changes slowly.
to envelope :x :t
  localmake "angle.a 360 * :waves.a * :x / :width - :t
  localmake "angle.b 360 * :waves.b * :x / :width - :t * 1.15
  output abs (2 * :height * cos ((:angle.a - :angle.b) / 2))
end

to neg.envelope :x :t
  output minus envelope :x :t
end

to labels
  setpc 0 setlabelheight 16
  penup setxy -360 250 label [blue wave]
  setxy -360 110 label [red wave]
  setxy -360 -40 label [blue + red]
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground 7
make "t 0
forever [
  clean
  labels
  draw.curve "wave.a 200 :t 1
  draw.curve "wave.b 60 :t 4
  draw.curve "envelope -170 :t 15
  draw.curve "neg.envelope -170 :t 15
  draw.curve "sum.ab -170 :t 0
  make "t :t + 6
  wait 2
]
